  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nQuiescent conditions will persist across the Mid-South in the\nnear term. The synoptic pattern across the CONUS is highlighted\nby a muted ridge over the Desert Southwest and troughing along\nthe East Coast. The resultant northwest flow aloft has allowed a\ndry air mass to set up camp over the Mid-South through the end of\nthe work week. This will support larger-than-normal diurnal\ntemperature ranges. Low temperatures will start near normal (mid\n50s to around 60 F) midweek, gradually climbing into the low/mid\n60s by the weekend. High temperatures will be above normal\nthroughout this time frame, spanning the 80s today and gradually\nclimbing into the mid 80s to lower 90s Thursday and Friday. Dry\nfuels and low relative humidity will result in elevated fire\ndanger, but wind speeds will generally remain light, limiting the\noverall impact.\n\nThe primary forecast concern today is the evolution and track of\nthe tropical disturbance over the Bay of Campeche. This system is\nexpected to intensify over the next few days, eventually lifting\nnorth toward the Gulf Coast this weekend. The track of this\nsystem will be heavily influenced by the subtropical ridge over\nBermuda and the evolution of the weak upper-level low currently\nover the Great Basin. The western upper low is forecast to drift\nsoutheast over TX by Friday, opening the door for the tropical\nsystem to lift poleward. There are still some fairly significant\ntiming differences between the ensemble systems, but the general\ntrend in the guidance suggests this feature will approach the\ncentral Gulf Coast over the weekend. It's worth noting that a few\nLREF members even hold this system back into early next week, so\nconfidence is pretty low at the moment. That said, a slower\npoleward acceleration would likely shunt this system farther east\nof the aforementioned trough swings through the Lower MS Valley\nSunday.\n\nThe main impacts associated with this tropical system will likely\nbe confined to the heavy rainfall potential. Moisture is forecast\nto surge into the region Saturday, with precipitable water\nexceeding 1.50\". This is just above the 90th percentile of\nclimatology for early October. Large-scale lift associated with\nthe trough to our west, combined with the anomalous moisture,\nwill support high PoPs and the potential for locally heavy\nrainfall, mainly east of the Mississippi River. Current NBM\nprobabilities for 2+ inches of rain exceed 50% across portions of\nnortheast MS this weekend, with 3\" probabilities near 30%.\nRainfall totals are likely to diminish farther west, with areas\nalong and west of the Mississippi River only sitting at 30-40%\nchance of 1\" or more. We'll have more information as this system\ndevelops.\n\nWe do anticipate a cold front arriving by early next week,\nbringing drier and cooler air to the region.",
